  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n- ${x}^{\u2032}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)$\n- ${x}^{\u2032}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)$\n- ${x}^{\u2032}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]x(t)$",
  "worked_solution": "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{\u2032}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nIn matrix form, a linear system ${x}^{\u2032}(t)=Ax(t)$ is decoupled if $A$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- Systems I and III are decoupled since their corresponding matrices are diagonal.\n\n- System II is *not* decoupled because its matrix is not diagonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\".",
